From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Raise test_performance_with_many_keys timeout for slower
 hosts

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [the 10s ceiling is sized for upstream's x86 CI; the riscv64 runners finish the same bounded work in about 12s]

The test builds a 5000-key summary case and lets pytest-benchmark run
summary.x over it. A call that takes longer than max_time (1s) makes
pytest-benchmark do exactly 2 calibration calls plus min_rounds (5),
so the work is a fixed 7 calls. On x86_64 one call takes about 0.1s
and the whole test about 1.2s, well inside its 10s marker. On the
riscv64 runners one call takes about 1.4s: the timeout fired after
the sixth call had printed its table, during the seventh and last.

The profile is linear in the number of keys (one SummaryKeyType
lookup per key); nothing loops. Raise the ceiling to 60s so it still
catches a real regression (a quadratic key lookup blows far past it)
without failing on a slower host.

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
 tests/test_view_summary.py | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tests/test_view_summary.py b/tests/test_view_summary.py
index 91ae42c..dca1053 100644
--- a/tests/test_view_summary.py
+++ b/tests/test_view_summary.py
@@ -855,7 +855,7 @@ def test_that_restart_and_base_times_are_concated(capsys):


 @pytest.mark.usefixtures("use_tmpdir")
-@pytest.mark.timeout(10)
+@pytest.mark.timeout(60)
 def test_performance_with_many_keys(monkeypatch, benchmark):
     subdir = Path("subdir")
     subdir.mkdir()
